A||||10||qwikgta||Poly Bushings...any tricks on installing them||||1||
Z||000000||qwikgta||05-07-2001||10:36 PM||||I have a full set of front and rear bushings for my 88 coupe and I will be installing them soon.  I remember that there are some tricks to making the install go smoother but don't remember what they are.  Any input.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tigger||05-07-2001||11:06 PM||||To install, first I removed the inner sleeves of the polys.  Lubed the outer of the bushing and then hammered them in using a piece of wood and hammer.  Lubed the inner sleeve then hammered those in the same.  I found a c-clamp with blocks of wood also works good to drive them all the way home.  <P>Read up on the writeup in <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/ogre" TARGET=_blank>Ogre's Cave</A> on bushings, good info there.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 05-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||05-07-2001||11:06 PM||||I took a dremel with a small grinding bit and ground small wells on the inner surface that comes in contact with the sleeve.  The theory is the grease will stay inside the bushing well and there will be no squeaks.  LowCG told me about this trick awhile back.<P>Burning out the old bushings is the best way to remove them.  Get a good fire going so the inside of the bushing melts away.  Be carefull because the metal sleeve will shoot out if you heat it too fast.  Walk away and let it burn for five minutes or so.  Then take a screwdriver and pry out the rest.  If you're lucky, the bushing will come out in one rubbery chunk.<P>You may have to heat the upper front control arm pivot bushings to get them in.  The fit is kind of tight.  Just go slow.  Plasic melts quicker than rubber.||||reg||1
Z||000003||qwikgta||05-07-2001||11:07 PM||||Thanks...will do, figures the Ogre would have this covered, should have looked there first.<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||05-08-2001||11:37 AM||||Here's a trick I used to get the rubber out of the front lower control arms that seemed to work well.<P>- Put a dent puller (mine is a cheap $5 one) through the bushing with a large washer on the far side.<BR>- Heat the outer sleeve uniformly with a torch until smoke/flame starts shooting out the ends. This took about 30 seconds with my little propane/oxygen torch. I'm not sure if a just propane will get it hot enough or not.<BR>- Blow the flame out if the rubber is burning.<BR>- Pop the dent puller a time or two and the rubber part should come right out with 99 + percent of the rubber in one piece.<BR>- Clean up the little bit of remaining rubber with a utility knife then a 1" sanding drum.<P>* Be careful, some bits of hot rubber may go flying when you pull it out. DEFINITELY wear safety glasses!<P>||||reg||3
Z||000005||Will||05-08-2001||12:23 PM||||I just cleaned out a full car set of 12 sleeves in preparation for making my own delrin bushings.<BR>I used a hole saw to cut in halfway from each direction. Once the inner sleeve and core of rubber were removed, the rest of the rubber came out easily on most of them, leaving a pristine surface and no mess on the inside of the outer sleeve.<BR>The adhesive which bonds the rubber to the outer sleeve had deteriorated on 10 of the 12. That's why I was able to pull the rubber out by hand once I removed the inner core.<BR>I only had to use a torch on the remaining two.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierohoho||05-08-2001||03:39 PM||||I found an easy way to remove the bushings using a dril and a utility knife.<BR> First remove the rubber lip from the end of the old bushing.<BR> Second, using a 1/8" drill bit in a drill, drill down along side the old bushing between it and the wall.<BR> Once the bit is in all the way, with the drill spinning the bit let the bit "walk" its way around the outside diameter of the old bushing.<BR> When it does this you will see the old bushing begin to come out on its own.<BR> No heat or hammerin.<BR> Worked for me on 4 rear a-arm bushings without a hitch. Good luck||||reg||1
Z||000007||qwikgta||05-08-2001||09:33 PM||||You all answered about getting the old rubber out, I never new that was difficult, my ?? was more on getting the poly in.  I seem to remember something about putting them in the frige or something.  Is the poly going in an easy thing.  Thanks<P>Rob<P>------------------<BR>qwikgta@yahoo.com<BR>88 T-Top coupe<BR>http://robsfieroproject.homestead.com/index.html<BR>http://vafiero.com||||reg||7
Z||000008||Eric_PA||05-08-2001||11:03 PM||||I had to jump in and say that using the drill bit works great. These bushings are hard to get out. To bad I didn't discover this until the last two. I was ticked and decided to go at it with a drill. It slipped and went into the edge of the bushing and what you know. It cut all around and just about fell out. This does work, but did mark up the sleeve a bit. Nothing a piece of sandpaper can't fix.<BR>Eric||||reg||6
Z||000009||Nashco||05-09-2001||02:44 AM||||I just want to keep this up top, because I am about to install a lot of poly.  So we now know tips for removal, thanks to everybody for that.  Sounds like I was right..heat seems like a lot of unnecessary messy work.<P>So, anybody have tips for install?  I seem to recall reading something about freezing them also, so that they would contract a little.<P>Thanks in advance, you guys and gals are a ton of help!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierohoho||05-10-2001||10:54 PM||||qwikgta, sorry bout that.<P> I did mine with the grease supplied by prothane and a rubber hammer.<P> It took about 3-4 minutes pounding per bushing and then the sleeves went in the same way only faster.<P> With the rubber hammer if you miss, it don't hurt as much on the hands and won't mark up or dent your piece.<P>Note: I did not put the piece in a vise or rest it on a work bench I free held it.<P>Good Luck!||||reg||3
